<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elliott Cresson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1796–1854</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elliott Cresson was a Quaker merchant and philanthropist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and a leading advocate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, which settled free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. He toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the early 1830s to raise funds for the cause. Port Cresson, the settlement that became Bassa Cove in Liberia, was named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elliott-cresson.jpg" alt="Elliott Cresson" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Cornelius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elliott_Cresson_photo_Restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
